he  geometry work that we do is in the 3d  world and that makes it handy  it's coordinate free definition is the  product of the magnitudes the magnitude  at least prior to the magnitudes times  the sine of the angle between which  means it goes to 0 when they're parallel  and that's a condition that indicates we  need to change something typically and  there's the definition and the first  little bit of Mathematica code proof of  orthogonality for the if we take the  cross product there's the algebra form  and if we take the dot product of each  of the original vectors we get 0 0  indicating that they're perpendicular ok  so some basic applications we can  project a point onto a parametric line  that is the set of all points some fixed  point Q plus multiples of a unit vector  in the direction of the line in a very  simple way it's just the scalar  projection of the difference between the  points that is the vector that goes from  Q to P projected scalar projector gives  me the length and vector projected give  me the component in that direction and  we can get distance points line with the  Pythagorean theorem on that that  triangle  okay so basic applications for planes we  can do distance or a point to plane very  very efficiently and we'll see how we  make use of that it's kind of the same  idea  we look at the difference between the  planes point Q and the point that's not  possibly not on the plane dotted with  the unit vector normal to the plane very  simple we can project a point on to a  plane simply by pushing it down by its  height above or below the plane nicely  this gives a signed distance positive  above the plane and negative below the  plane and intersecting a line with the  plane left talk about this a little  later it has a nice tight formulation  that we can actually discover using  Mathematica symbolic capability so a  nice full blown application real tight  code using cross-product and dot product  to intersect an ellipse with a plane  when yo