ave to look at all the points  all the vertices in the mesh but you  want to do that in a way that's very  light and what we ended up doing is  simply looking at the sign of the  distance of the vertices from the plane  and making sure that the only ones we  selected out of the vertices were ones  where some of the points were above the  plane some were below and that again is  a nice one line solution I don't know  why my pictures not showing up  there we go okay  so there's the vertices selected the  polygon selected whose vertices are on  both sides of the plane and so we  reduced the difficult problem to a  simple problem  okay so now we simply intersect those  polygons the line edges of those  polygons to find the loops in the plane  and  yeah so there's our sliced Triceratops  and of course now that we know where  those intersection lines are polygons  that are constructed that way we could  cut there and we actually do in the  application and similar techniques you  can actually intersect the cylinder with  a general mesh object  yeah  we can play the same trick to select the  polygons of interest by looking at their  distance from the cylinder axis which is  again the chief calculation and  selecting the ones with the right radius  and then performing the lines  intersection  another little application that we came  across  not heavy use of vector geometry but  simulating a fabry-perot interferometer  for a customer where the two variables  are the angle C the ya the angular  relative angle between the two prism  elements and the distance between them  that was nice and easy in Mathematica  code as well the basic components of  this or refraction and reflection and of  course we use the cross product to build  up the normal vectors to the surfaces in  an efficient way  a development method note this turned  out to work sometimes if we have a  layered set of functions nested  functions where we call the outer  function with symbolic vectors and  scalars nice 